<h2>The prosecution story</h2>
<p>On the evening of 22 July 2011, near a red light by the MCD office in Janak Puri, two men allegedly robbed the complainant and his friends at knifepoint. A &ldquo;chor chor&rdquo; alarm was raised; one of the fleeing men was said to have fallen and broken a tooth; passing police allegedly caught both men on the spot, recovering a phone and cash from one and a phone, cash and the knife from the other. Eleven witnesses were examined. The trial court acquitted both accused in January 2015, giving them the benefit of doubt, and the State obtained leave to appeal in 2018.</p>

<h2>The appellate standard</h2>
<p>The judgment opens its analysis with the two guardrails of acquittal appeals, drawn from the Supreme Court&rsquo;s decisions in <em>Chandrappa</em> (2007) and <em>Mallappa</em> (2024), both cited in the order: an acquittal strengthens the presumption of innocence, and the appellate court does not substitute its own view for a reasonably possible one taken below.</p>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old"><strong>What the State needed.</strong> To show the trial court&rsquo;s reading of the evidence was impossible or perverse — that no reasonable court could have acquitted on this record.</div>
  <div class="arrow">&rarr;</div>
  <div class="col new"><strong>What the record showed.</strong> Contradictions on who fell, who was caught, what was recovered from whom, where the documents were prepared, and who was present — each undermining the apprehension-and-recovery narrative.</div>
</div>

<h2>Where the case unravelled</h2>
<p>The complainant corrected himself mid-deposition on which accused fell during the chase and which recovery belonged to whom. A second alleged victim did not support the apprehension of one accused or the knife recovery from the other. The complainant&rsquo;s father, cast as present at the scene, deposed that he was elsewhere and reached the police station later — and his account of a second arrest from a market conflicted with the version of an on-the-spot double apprehension. The police witnesses themselves diverged on who was present during the proceedings. The court held these were not the minor variations natural to honest witnesses; they went to the core, and with the recoveries unreliable, nothing safe remained to anchor identity. Para 51 states the conclusion: &ldquo;the learned Trial Court adopted a reasonably possible view of the evidence.&rdquo;</p>

<h2>What this means in practice</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>For the defence.</strong> Cross-examination that fixes each witness to specifics — who, where, what, in whose presence — is what makes contradictions &ldquo;material&rdquo; rather than cosmetic. This case is a study in how spot-arrest narratives are tested.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>For the prosecution.</strong> Street-crime cases tried years after the event stand or fall on the consistency of the first-day paperwork and the recovery evidence. Where the documents&rsquo; place of preparation is itself disputed, the benefit of doubt follows.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>On timelines.</strong> Offence in 2011, acquittal in 2015, leave in 2018, appellate judgment in 2026 — a reminder that acquittal appeals move slowly, and that the double presumption of innocence grows heavier with time.</div>
</div>
